    "difficultyReason": "Part a is a one-step complement (1 - 1/3), which is easy. Part b sets the demand. The student must see that 'what is left' is 2/3 of the existing 3/4, not 2/3 of a whole tank. They must then choose multiplication of fractions and cancel 3 with 3 and 2 with 4. That is a two-stage fraction-of-a-fraction context with a small representation shift, and nothing is hidden or unsignposted. It is harder than p99-q2, which has a single inverse fraction step. It is easier than p102-q18, which chains four factors, rewrites each 1 - 1/n, and spots the telescoping cancellation. It is far below the four-stage monkey problem p60-q10, which asks for the fraction of the original left after four successive 'eats'.",
